      "prompt_tokens": 18,
      "completion_tokens": 1244,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical substance that is essential for life on Earth. It is a transparent, odorless, and tasteless liquid.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, ranging from its chemistry to its physical properties:\n\n### 1. Chemical Composition\nWater is a chemical compound with the formula **$H_2O$**. This means that each molecule of water is made up of:\n*   **Two hydrogen atoms**\n*   **One oxygen atom**\n\nThese atoms are chemically bonded together. The oxygen atom is larger and more electronegative than the hydrogen atoms, which gives water a slight electrical charge. This makes water a **polar molecule**, meaning it has a positive end and a negative end. This polarity is what allows water to stick to itself and dissolve other substances.\n\n### 2. Physical States\nWater is unique because it can exist naturally in all three states of matter under standard conditions on Earth:\n*   **Solid:** When frozen, it becomes ice.\n*   **Liquid:** This is the state we encounter most often (rain, oceans, drinking water).\n*   **Gas:** When heated, it turns into steam or water vapor.\n\n### 3. Unique Properties\nWater has several physical properties that make it unlike most other liquids:\n*   **The Universal Solvent:** Water is excellent at dissolving other substances. This is why it is the main component of blood and why it is used by plants to transport nutrients from the soil.\n*   **High Surface Tension:** Water molecules stick together very strongly at the surface, creating a kind of \"skin.\" This is why water forms droplets and why some insects can walk across the surface of a pond.\n*   **High Specific Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat without getting hot. This helps regulate the Earth's climate and keeps our bodies at a stable temperature.\n\n### 4. Abundance\nWater is the most abundant compound on the surface of the Earth. It covers about 71% of the planet. It is found in oceans, lakes, rivers, glaciers, underground aquifers, and in the atmosphere as clouds and humidity. It is also found inside every living cell of every plant and animal.\n\nIn summary, water is a simple molecule ($H_2O$) that acts as a universal solvent and a vital medium for biological life."
